# #1966 (PR 1 of the operator's two-PR "Option B, gated" plan, ruled
# 2026-09-18): scripts/claim-staleness.sh (and its test) carry the local-only
# frozen-timeline protection that the #668 pin above has so far kept as inline
# prose in curator.md/judge.md/doctor.md -- relocated into the shared script
# upstream's role files already drive, mirroring the #1546 pin's shape (an
# isolated script pin instead of whole role-file pins). When the `check`
# subcommand reaches a `stale`/`stale-bounded-fallback` verdict it now (a)
# re-reads the issue/PR resource itself (labels + updated_at, a NON-timeline
# read) and fails safe to `unknown` if that read fails or is rate-limited, and
# (b) runs a reclaim-loop guard: a trusted reclaim comment newer than
# CLAIMED_AT (the #668 `<!-- loom:reclaimed claim=... -->` marker or
# upstream's marker-less "Reclaiming ... claim" prose) plus a ground-truth
# updated_at past CLAIMED_AT means /timeline is frozen, so the verdict is
# downgraded to `fresh` (FROZEN_TIMELINE=true) -- one reclaim per frozen
# CLAIMED_AT instead of the 30+ that hit #509 on 2026-08-10. The test's
# T21-T30 cases reproduce #509 and the fail-safe paths. Upstream's
# defaults/scripts/claim-staleness.sh has no equivalent (verified 2026-10-02
# against rjwalters/loom @ 65442ba48: no `updatedAt`/`frozen`/`RECLAIM`
# anywhere in it), so an unpinned resync would silently revert it -- the same
# reversion pattern as #606/#662/#1292/#1316/#1356/#1546/#1674/#1898. The #668
# whole-file pins above stay until PR 2 of #1966 unpins them, which the
# operator gated on THIS pin landing and being verified. If the upstream Loom
# project ever adopts an equivalent frozen-timeline guard, these pins can be
# dropped and the files resynced normally.
scripts/claim-staleness.sh
scripts/tests/test-claim-staleness.sh

# #1749: champion-pr-merge.md carries a local-only propagation from a held
# PR's `loom:operator` state onto the issue(s) it would close — when the
# merge-risk hold (criterion #2) or the critical-file hold (criterion #3)
# applies `loom:operator` to a PR, the linked issue(s) (via
# `closingIssuesReferences`) are marked `loom:blocked` with a machine-
# parseable `Blocked by #<PR>` comment, and the release paths remove it again.
# Without this, the daemon's work-finder kept re-dispatching a no-op sweep on
# the issue every cycle even though its sole open PR was already held for
# human merge (`loom-daemon`'s `PARK_LABELS` hard-skips `loom:blocked`, but
# had no signal that the issue was, transitively, also stuck on a human) —
# confirmed recurring three times on issue #1651 / PR #1659 before this fix.
# This was never upstreamed into rjwalters/loom's own champion-pr-merge.md /
# label-state-machine.md templates, so an unpinned resync would silently drop
# it — the same reversion pattern as #606/#662/#1292/#1316/#1356/#1546/#1674.
# If the upstream Loom project ever adopts an equivalent propagation, these
# pins can be dropped and the files resynced normally. Re-verified 2026-09-27
# (#2549) against rjwalters/loom @ d1a43413: upstream's
# defaults/.claude/commands/loom/champion-pr-merge.md still has no `Blocked by
# #<PR>` / `loom:operator`-to-issue propagation, so the pin stays.
#
# #2549 BROADENS what this pin holds, so read the pin as "this file is now
# locally maintained", not just "it carries one extra propagation". Freezing
# champion-pr-merge.md at its 2026-09 baseline also froze the merge-step wiring
# for merge-pr.sh's newer exit codes, which kept moving upstream: exit 4
# (#8508, `--redate-stale-checks` performed the #8248 freshness guard's own
# remedy) and exit 5 (#8896, `--auto`'s bounded settle-wait expired) are
# re-queue signals, NOT merge failures, and the pinned copy's `MERGE_RC`
# branching only knew exit 3 — so a PR whose CI merely outran
# LOOM_AUTO_MERGE_TIMEOUT was narrated and labelled as a failed merge. The
# exit-4/exit-5 branches, the `Exception: exit codes 3, 4 and 5` section, and
# the link to `.loom/docs/merge-pr-exit-code-exceptions.md` (which supersedes
# the formerly-inline squash/merge-ancestry trap note) were hand-ported from
# upstream in #2549. `.loom/scripts/tests/test-merge-pr-head-mismatch.sh` is
# the oracle for all of this — it asserts the wiring against this pinned file,
# so when merge-pr.sh grows another exit code, that suite fails and the new
# branch must be hand-ported here too. Whoever eventually drops this pin must
# confirm the resynced upstream file still carries the exit-code wiring as
# well as an equivalent operator-state propagation.
commands/loom/champion-pr-merge.md
docs/label-state-machine.md

